From ae4412d1c85251ab8b7c4456fe042ad780757e87 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 14:24:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] lifecycle guard: walk heredoc-body producers piped
 into startup shells; stop brace/group over-block

Follow-up to PR #2928 review 5901902665 (card t_c5292838672dfefe).

1. Fail-open (pre-existing): `python3 - <<'PY' | bash -l` whose body prints a
   gateway/kanban.db action was not walked in the nested unknown-alias scope:
   _printed_pipeline_units yielded heredoc body lines as separate pipe-less
   units. Heredoc bodies now join the printed-text unit that owns their opener,
   and are walked when that producer is upstream of the startup-shell pipe.
2. Fail-closed over-block (narrow): a literal `{` argument (`echo {`) no longer
   opens a group (only reserved words in command position do), and a list
   operator or newline inside a group ends that group's pipe, so a later
   `bash -l` in `{ printf X | cat; bash -l; }` reads the group's stdin, not
   printf's output. `if/while/until/for ... fi/done | bash -l` and multi-line
   groups piped to a login shell are now recognised as one unit too.

Proof:
- tests/cron/test_lifecycle_guard_session_aliases.py: 110 passed; with
  cron/lifecycle_guard_nested_shells.py reverted to 03bc34ee0c, 18 new cases
  fail (9 heredoc-producer, 5 over-block, 4 group-output).
- All 26 tests/cron/*lifecycle* files: 2023 passed.
- Replay vs 03bc34ee0c over the frozen 34579-command worker-1 corpus, alias
